WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.029
Monday. Your AI agent saves a report.

2
00:00:04.579 --> 00:00:06.146
Company revenue: five thousand,

3
00:00:06.186 --> 00:00:09.207
five hundred sixty-five dollars.

4
00:00:09.757 --> 00:00:11.666
Tuesday, someone corrects the euro

5
00:00:11.706 --> 00:00:12.706
exchange rate.

6
00:00:13.251 --> 00:00:14.914
The report doesn't change.

7
00:00:15.164 --> 00:00:17.044
Nothing tells it that it's wrong.

8
00:00:18.350 --> 00:00:20.420
Agents build on their own saved work,

9
00:00:20.490 --> 00:00:23.270
so every number quietly depends on inputs

10
00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:24.310
underneath.

11
00:00:24.542 --> 00:00:26.580
When one input changes,

12
00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:28.573
which numbers are now wrong?

13
00:00:28.823 --> 00:00:31.019
Re-reading everything floods the model

14
00:00:31.059 --> 00:00:32.300
with context.

15
00:00:32.550 --> 00:00:35.645
Searching for related text misses numbers

16
00:00:35.685 --> 00:00:37.845
that depend on the change but never

17
00:00:37.885 --> 00:00:39.396
mention it.

18
00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:42.952
ProofFlow takes a different path.

19
00:00:43.202 --> 00:00:45.896
Every number keeps its receipts:

20
00:00:46.046 --> 00:00:48.226
exactly which inputs, and which

21
00:00:48.266 --> 00:00:50.000
calculation, made it.

22
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:51.981
So it knows what to hold back,

23
00:00:52.021 --> 00:00:54.102
and what to redo.

24
00:00:56.050 --> 00:00:58.652
An input, like the exchange rate,

25
00:00:58.862 --> 00:01:00.688
is saved as a version.

26
00:01:00.938 --> 00:01:03.834
A tool reads it, and leaves a receipt.

27
00:01:04.084 --> 00:01:06.296
The receipt points to its result,

28
00:01:06.386 --> 00:01:09.166
and each number in a report points to that

29
00:01:09.206 --> 00:01:10.206
result.

30
00:01:10.529 --> 00:01:12.360
Now, correct the rate.

31
00:01:12.810 --> 00:01:14.980
The change travels up those links.

32
00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.380
Both revenue figures are withheld,

33
00:01:17.470 --> 00:01:18.772
never shown stale.

34
00:01:19.022 --> 00:01:20.693
Support never read the rate,

35
00:01:20.733 --> 00:01:22.086
so it stays.

36
00:01:22.336 --> 00:01:25.498
Refresh re-runs only the affected steps.

37
00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:29.865
This is ProofFlow, running today.

38
00:01:30.115 --> 00:01:32.084
Upload orders as a CSV,

39
00:01:32.535 --> 00:01:35.618
preview them, and create the reports.

40
00:01:35.868 --> 00:01:37.538
Publish a corrected rate.

41
00:01:37.828 --> 00:01:39.508
Affected figures are withheld,

42
00:01:39.548 --> 00:01:41.762
and the evidence path shows why.

43
00:01:42.012 --> 00:01:43.949
Refresh brings them current,

44
00:01:44.119 --> 00:01:46.499
and a checked report can only be copied

45
00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.892
while every figure is current.

46
00:01:49.142 --> 00:01:50.462
Agents get the same power,

47
00:01:50.502 --> 00:01:52.931
through five MCP tools.

48
00:01:54.450 --> 00:01:56.368
Under the hood, it's small,

49
00:01:56.408 --> 00:01:57.943
and strict.

50
00:01:58.393 --> 00:02:00.283
At the center is the runtime.

51
00:02:00.973 --> 00:02:02.913
Every tool run produces an immutable

52
00:02:02.953 --> 00:02:05.302
result, and a receipt.

53
00:02:05.552 --> 00:02:08.153
Content goes into a store that verifies

54
00:02:08.193 --> 00:02:09.779
every hash.

55
00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:12.171
Relationships go into HydraDB,

56
00:02:12.211 --> 00:02:14.358
as a lineage graph.

57
00:02:14.608 --> 00:02:15.923
When a correction arrives,

58
00:02:16.053 --> 00:02:18.653
HydraDB walks that graph natively,

59
00:02:18.693 --> 00:02:21.264
to find everything downstream.

60
00:02:21.514 --> 00:02:24.399
The runtime re-runs stale steps in order,

61
00:02:24.709 --> 00:02:26.419
reuses what's current,

62
00:02:26.789 --> 00:02:28.691
and records new receipts.

63
00:02:28.941 --> 00:02:30.331
If evidence is missing,

64
00:02:30.621 --> 00:02:33.131
it fails closed. No fallback.

65
00:02:33.561 --> 00:02:34.561
No guess.

66
00:02:34.667 --> 00:02:36.762
Around it: a browser app,

67
00:02:36.832 --> 00:02:40.089
a Python API, and an MCP server.

68
00:02:40.339 --> 00:02:43.135
Agents choose plans from typed tools,

69
00:02:43.285 --> 00:02:45.647
but never run arbitrary code.

70
00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:54.950
Does it work?

71
00:02:55.311 --> 00:02:58.127
In a frozen study with a real model,

72
00:02:58.457 --> 00:03:00.477
ProofFlow refreshed all thirty-two

73
00:03:00.517 --> 00:03:03.957
affected reports, matching full context at

74
00:03:03.997 --> 00:03:06.376
under forty percent of its input.

75
00:03:06.626 --> 00:03:08.736
Keyword search managed twenty-five.

76
00:03:08.806 --> 00:03:10.810
Vector search, five.

77
00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.558
On a retail benchmark:

78
00:03:12.768 --> 00:03:13.868
thirty-eight of thirty-eight,

79
00:03:13.908 --> 00:03:16.428
with sixty-nine percent fewer evidence

80
00:03:16.468 --> 00:03:17.673
tokens.

81
00:03:17.923 --> 00:03:20.653
A plain dependency walk ties it.

82
00:03:21.043 --> 00:03:23.713
The point is recording dependencies.

83
00:03:25.450 --> 00:03:26.450
ProofFlow.

84
00:03:26.576 --> 00:03:28.276
Saved reports that know when they're out

85
00:03:28.316 --> 00:03:29.316
of date.

86
00:03:29.367 --> 00:03:31.037
The sample data is authored.

87
00:03:31.287 --> 00:03:32.867
Every result shown is in the public

88
00:03:32.907 --> 00:03:33.907
evidence.
